<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<ruleset name="Exeris PMD Ruleset — TCK"
    xmlns="http://pmd.sourceforge.net/ruleset/2.0.0"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://pmd.sourceforge.net/ruleset/2.0.0 http://pmd.sourceforge.net/ruleset_2_0_0.xsd">

    <description>
        The main Exeris ruleset MINUS a named list, for exeris-kernel-tck.

        That module's main sources are contract tests: abstract JUnit classes a provider
        subclasses. They live in src/main because they are the module's published API, not because
        they are production runtime code — so the rules whose premise is "this is production code"
        do not describe them, while the rules that catch a broken test still do.

        Written as a reference to the main ruleset rather than as a copy, deliberately: a rule added
        to pmd-ruleset.xml applies here too unless somebody excludes it on purpose, and the
        difference between the two files stays readable as a list.

        Every exclusion below was measured on the module, not anticipated: 1782 violations across 56
        rules when the classes were first compiled as main sources. What is NOT excluded is the
        point of the file — those findings were fixed in the code instead.
    </description>

    <rule ref="pmd-ruleset.xml">

        <!--
            1. Test naming and test shape.

            A TCK case is named for the clause it pins, and reads as a sentence:
            `close_after_close_is_idempotent`. MethodNamingConventions wants camelCase and would
            make every one of them worse. The same holds for the rest of this group — a @Nested
            class per contract clause, fields declared beside the case that uses them, and one-letter
            lambda parameters in an assertion are how a readable suite is written.

            UnitTestContainsTooManyAsserts (331 hits) is the sharpest example of the mismatch: a
            contract test asserting eight clauses of one contract is doing its job. Splitting it
            into eight cases would hide which clauses belong together.
        -->
        <exclude name="MethodNamingConventions"/>
        <exclude name="ShortMethodName"/>
        <exclude name="ShortClassName"/>
        <exclude name="ShortVariable"/>
        <exclude name="LinguisticNaming"/>
        <exclude name="CommentDefaultAccessModifier"/>
        <exclude name="FieldDeclarationsShouldBeAtStartOfClass"/>
        <exclude name="UnitTestContainsTooManyAsserts"/>
        <exclude name="UnitTestAssertionsShouldIncludeMessage"/>
        <exclude name="ControlStatementBraces"/>
        <exclude name="UseExplicitTypes"/>
        <exclude name="AvoidDuplicateLiterals"/>
        <exclude name="UnnecessaryFullyQualifiedName"/>
        <exclude name="LambdaCanBeMethodReference"/>
        <exclude name="UseUnderscoresInNumericLiterals"/>
        <exclude name="RedundantFieldInitializer"/>
        <exclude name="NullAssignment"/>
        <exclude name="SingularField"/>
        <exclude name="LooseCoupling"/>
        <exclude name="LawOfDemeter"/>

        <!--
            2. UnitTestShouldIncludeAssert cannot see through one level of indirection.

            Verified on two independent samples rather than assumed. `void memory() {
            audit(SubsystemUnderTest.MEMORY); }` asserts inside `audit`; `asTenant(TENANT_A, () ->
            { ... })` asserts inside a lambda handed to a helper. PMD reports both as
            assertion-free. Sharing setup through a helper is exactly how a TCK avoids repeating
            the same six lines in forty cases, so this rule is structurally wrong here rather than
            occasionally wrong.

            The same false positive is on record for SonarCloud's java:S2187 against @Nested-only
            classes in this repository.
        -->
        <exclude name="UnitTestShouldIncludeAssert"/>

        <!--
            3. Things a contract test does on purpose, which production code must not.

            CloseResource (81 hits): a TCK that asserts the KERNEL closed something must not close
            it itself — `TrackedEngine graph = kernel.graph(); assertThat(graph.isClosed())` is the
            whole case. AbstractLoanedBufferTck deliberately drives unbalanced acquire/close to
            prove the leak detector fires.

            Catching and throwing broadly is how a contract test states "any failure here is a
            failure of the contract"; forcing GC is how an allocation TCK reaches a stable
            measurement; 127.0.0.1 is what a loopback TCK dials; allocating in a loop is what a
            churn TCK is measuring.
        -->
        <exclude name="CloseResource"/>
        <exclude name="UseTryWithResources"/>
        <exclude name="AvoidCatchingGenericException"/>
        <exclude name="AvoidThrowingRawExceptionTypes"/>
        <exclude name="AvoidUncheckedExceptionsInSignatures"/>
        <exclude name="SignatureDeclareThrowsException"/>
        <exclude name="DoNotCallGarbageCollectionExplicitly"/>
        <exclude name="AvoidUsingHardCodedIP"/>
        <exclude name="AvoidInstantiatingObjectsInLoops"/>
        <exclude name="AvoidLiteralsInIfCondition"/>
        <exclude name="AssignmentInOperand"/>

        <!--
            JUnit5TestShouldBePackagePrivate is not merely inconvenient here, it is impossible to
            satisfy: these @Test methods are declared on abstract classes in
            eu.exeris.kernel.tck.contract and INHERITED by bindings in eu.exeris.kernel.community
            and elsewhere. A package-private method is not visible to a subclass in another package,
            so following this rule would stop the TCK being a TCK.
        -->
        <exclude name="JUnit5TestShouldBePackagePrivate"/>

        <!--
            EmptyControlStatement: the RAII payload protocol is verified by a handler whose whole
            body is `try (p) { /* no-op */ }` — receiving the payload and closing it is the contract
            being asserted. Unlike EmptyCatchBlock, this rule has no allowCommentedBlocks property,
            so the comment that explains the emptiness cannot reach it.

            CompareObjectsWithEquals: `if (thread != self)` is identity comparison on Thread, where
            identity IS the semantics — and the persistence TCK likewise asserts flyweight identity,
            which equals() would hide.
        -->
        <exclude name="EmptyControlStatement"/>
        <exclude name="CompareObjectsWithEquals"/>

        <!--
            4. Size and complexity, measured against the wrong unit.

            These rules bound a production class. The unit here is a CONTRACT: AbstractBlobStorageTck
            has one case per clause of the blob contract, and the clause count is set by the SPI
            rather than by the author. Splitting a suite to satisfy TooManyMethods would scatter one
            contract across several files and make the missing clause harder to see, which is the
            opposite of what the number is for.
        -->
        <exclude name="TooManyMethods"/>
        <exclude name="CyclomaticComplexity"/>
        <exclude name="CognitiveComplexity"/>
        <exclude name="NPathComplexity"/>
        <exclude name="ExcessiveParameterList"/>
        <exclude name="ExcessiveImports"/>
        <exclude name="AvoidDeeplyNestedIfStmts"/>
        <exclude name="SimplifyBooleanReturns"/>
        <exclude name="EmptyMethodInAbstractClassShouldBeAbstract"/>
        <exclude name="AbstractClassWithoutAbstractMethod"/>
        <exclude name="AbstractClassWithoutAnyMethod"/>

        <!--
            5. Performance rules written for a runtime hot path.

            StringBuilder append patterns matter where the kernel builds a response per request.
            A TCK builds a failure message once, on the path where the test is already failing.
        -->
        <exclude name="ConsecutiveAppendsShouldReuse"/>
        <exclude name="ConsecutiveLiteralAppends"/>
        <exclude name="AppendCharacterWithChar"/>

        <!--
            NOT excluded, and this half is the reason the file exists rather than a `pmd.skip`:

              UnusedLocalVariable          — a value computed and never asserted is a test that
                                             does less than it reads as doing
              UnusedImports / UnnecessaryImport
              UnnecessaryWarningSuppression — a suppression that no longer suppresses anything
              CompareObjectsWithEquals     — `==` in an assertion is how a test passes against the
                                             wrong object
              EmptyControlStatement        — an `if` with no body in a test is a missing assertion
              LiteralsFirstInComparisons   — an NPE in a test reports as an error, not a failure
              UseLocaleWithCaseConversions — locale-dependent case folding is wrong in a contract
              UseProperClassLoader
              TestClassWithoutTestCases
              EmptyCatchBlock              — inherited with the main ruleset's `ignored|expected`
                                             allowance

            Each of these fired on the module and was fixed in the code rather than excluded here.
        -->
    </rule>

    <!--
        TestClassWithoutTestCases fired once, on `TestFlowContexts` — a package-private FlowContext
        factory that is a fixture, not a test. The rule keys on the class NAME, and its default
        pattern treats a leading `Test` as a promise of test cases. Narrowing the pattern to this
        module's actual convention (`*Tck`, `*Test`) keeps the rule live for the shape it is for —
        a suite class that ended up with no cases — instead of switching it off.
    -->
    <rule ref="category/java/errorprone.xml/TestClassWithoutTestCases">
        <properties>
            <property name="testClassPattern" value="^(\w+Tck|\w+Test(s|Case)?)$"/>
        </properties>
    </rule>
</ruleset>
